BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR OUR MAY FICTION BOOK CLUB:
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260511T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260512T203000
UID:bm-5151020260511@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Copy_of_f
 uture_present__1_.png?cb=1777944098
DESCRIPTION:[Book Club]\nWe’ve been looking forward to this one - Olga T
 okarczuk’s\,\nHouse of Day\, House of Night\n(translated by Antonia Lloy
 d-Jones) is the kind of book you don’t quite shake\, and we can’t wait
  to talk about it with you.\nFrom the Nobel Prize–winning\,\nNew York Ti
 mes\nbestselling author of T\nhe Books of Jacob and Drive Your Plow Over t
 he Bones of the Dead\,\nthis is a stunning novel about the quiet strangene
 ss - and richness - of small places.\nHouse of Day\, House of Night\nis wo
 nderfully funny and intelligent—a vivid\, fictional portrait of a place 
 and its inhabitants.\nJoin us on Monday\, May 11 or Tuesday\, May 12 for s
 ome great conversation. We hope to see you there.\nYour book club ticket i
 ncludes:\nA glass of wine (or another beverage of your choice).\nA thought
 ful conversation with our brilliant moderator\, Lee Suksi.\nA $20 credit t
 oward any book in the shop.\nGET YOUR TICKETS HERE!\nA few details:\nTicke
 t price includes HST and fees.\n$20 book credit is valid on the night of a
 ttendance only (non-refundable and non-transferable).\nWine is served on t
 he night of the event (ID required).\nTickets are non-refundable within 7 
 days of the event\; due to limited capacity and planning\, we’re not abl
 e to transfer tickets to future book club sessions.
LOCATION:Flying Books at Neverland\, 371 Queen St. W.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US THE LAUNCH OF THE TIME OF THE GREAT SINGING BY ELIZABETH P
 HILIPS
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260512T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260512T200000
UID:bm-5019020260512@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Elizabeth
 _Philips_photo_credit_D.W._Larson.jpg?cb=1776178498
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nFreehand Books and Flying Books is thrilled to 
 host the launch of Elizabeth Philip's new book\,\nThe Time of the Great Si
 nging.\nShe will be joined by Pujita Verma\, author of\nPrecedence\,\nwith
  Lee Gowan moderating.\nA kinetic new poetry collection that sings our ast
 onishment and our fear at how life makes and unmakes us through every love
  and every grief.\n\n\n\n\nThese poems are at once plangent and wise\, fir
 ed by exacting observation of the changes that mark the natural world—th
 e diminishing woods\, the surging birdsong in spring–as well as each sta
 ge of human life. The poet struggles to understand what her experiences me
 an\, even as a sudden turn—a mother’s illness\, a father’s death—s
 weeps her in an entirely different direction.\nPhilips writes of “this b
 orrowed ground\,” our trespass on the earth none of us can own. In every
  movement of these poems as they flow over rough ground and smooth\, there
 ’s the thrill of discovery\, and the echoing notes of what we can’t kn
 ow\, the mystery of changes yet to come.\nElizabeth Philips’s first nove
 l\,\nThe Afterlife of Birds\n\, won the City of Saskatoon Book Award and w
 as a finalist for the Amazon Canada First Novel Award. She is the former e
 ditor of\nGrain\nmagazine\, and the former director of the Banff Centre’
 s Emerging Writers Intensive.\nThe Time of the Great Singing\nis her fifth
  book of poetry. She lives in Saskatoon.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College St.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN AGA MAKSIMOWSKA IN CONVERSATION WITH AMY JONES
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260514T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260514T200000
UID:bm-4910220260514@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Flying_Bo
 oks_Aga_and_Amy.jpg?cb=1774041660
DESCRIPTION:[Book Signing]\nDundurn Press and Flying Books are excited to 
 have Aga Maksimowska\, author of\nBecalming\n\, in conversation about her 
 book and mentorship with Amy Jones at our College St. location. Book signi
 ng to follow.\nABOUT THE BOOK:\nGosia feels stalled in her life\, but a fa
 mily trip opens her eyes to the life she has … and the life she wants.\n
 \nGosia\, a high school chemistry teacher\, travels to her native Poland t
 o visit her estranged father. Back in Canada\, meanwhile\, her father-in-l
 aw — who has been more of a dad to her than her own — is dying of canc
 er. Away from her routine\, Gosia questions everything in her life\, inclu
 ding her long-term boyfriend\, Peter. She feels stuck in the terrifying ti
 me of early adulthood\, in her first grown-up job while managing student d
 ebt\, monogamy\, and existential dread. Is this really it\, she wonders?\n
 \nGosia’s time in Poland gives her the chance to examine her life\, and 
 she finds herself pulled homeward to Canada\, where she faces the fact tha
 t Peter’s father — like her own — is far from perfect. Can she love 
 despite betrayal? Can she find hope in her fiery\, complex love for Peter?
  Is there something more to this life that she didn’t even realize she h
 ad?\n\nBecalming tells the story of two people realizing that happily ever
  after is not something to be but something to continue to explore\, throu
 gh adversity and outrage\, tragedy and inspiration\, and love.\nABOUT THE 
 AUTHOR\nAga Maksimowska is a writer whose debut novel\nGiant\,\nabout prem
 ature sexual development and the fall of Communism in Poland\, was shortli
 sted for the Toronto Book Award. Her writing has appeared in\nPolish[ed]: 
 Poland Rooted in Canadian Fiction\, Brick\,\nt\nhe Globe and Mail\,\nand o
 thers. Becalming is her second novel. She lives in Toronto.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College St.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF SMALLER ANIMALS
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260519T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260519T200000
UID:bm-4793220260519@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/unnamed.j
 pg?cb=1773254238
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nJoin us for an evening of discussion on\nSmalle
 r Animals\n\, the debut novel by Canadian author Carter Vance\, in convers
 ation with Doug Vidal-Hernandez.\nAdam Brinkert was raised a world apart f
 rom the lights of Ottawa and the din of politics\, in a small city in Nort
 hern Ontario. Pursuing justice and a way to make things better for the peo
 ple he grew up with\, he became an assistant to a Member of Parliament\, l
 iving his youth in the offices and hallways of power. But\, as he grows to
 wards his 30s\, he begins to doubt what it was all for\; were his experien
 ces all merely for self-aggrandizement and not the greater good?\n\nTwists
  of fate and slights of political hand will test Adam’s belief in himsel
 f\, and what he believes is right\, while he navigates the landscape of a 
 country\, and a world\, that is changing rapidly. All the while\, he strug
 gles to define his relationships with the people around him\, including hi
 s erstwhile friend-cum-political rival Truss and his ex-girlfriend and co-
 worker Kathleen. Adam’s journey will take him across the county and back
  to his home\, searching for a spark to guide him where he truly belongs.\
 nSmaller Animals i\ns a story of living\, loving and growing up on Parliam
 ent Hill that introduces a powerful new voice in Canadian literature.\nCar
 ter Vance is a writer and poet originally from Cobourg\, Ontario\, current
 ly a resident in Gatineau\, Quebec. His work has appeared in such publicat
 ions as\nThe Smart Set\, Contemporary Verse 2\nand\nA Midwestern Review\,\
 namongst others.\nSmaller Animals\nis his first novel\, following two poet
 ry collections.\n\nCarter has worked as a researcher and policy analyst wi
 th government and non-profit organizations in Canada\, the United Kingdom\
 , Dominica and Indonesia. He was a staffer for several members of Parliame
 nt\, including the Opposition House Leader in 2014 and also worked in the 
 Senate of Canada.\nDoug Vidal-Hernández was a political staffer at the Na
 tional Office of the Liberal Party of Canada and worked through the 2015 n
 ational campaign as part of a rapid response team providing local candidat
 es with policy support. He then became part of the caucus services team at
  the Liberal Research Bureau\, supporting parliamentarians and their polit
 ical staff. \n\nNow\, as a recovered staffer he works at Code for Canada\,
  a national non profit focused on creating strong digital public services\
 , accessible and inclusive design and responsible AI use.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College St.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF LATERAL SWAY BY HANNAH KARPINSKI
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260520T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260520T200000
UID:bm-5027220260520@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/HK.JPG?cb
 =1775601294
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nPlease join Metatron and Flying Books for the l
 aunch of Hannah Karpinski’s new book\,\nLateral Sway.\nHannah will be jo
 ined by Jowita Bydlowska\, author of\nUnshaming.\nThe evening will be host
 ed by Ashley Obscura. Book signing to follow.\nMoving through queer intima
 cy\, memory\, and displacement\, the poems in this collection trace what i
 t means to be pulled off-course—towards others\, towards language\, towa
 rds fleeting forms of belonging. Fragmentary and tender\,\nLateral Sway\no
 ffers an account of how connections survive across bodies\, borders\, and 
 time.\nHannah Karpinski is a lesbian Canadian poet from Tkaronto/Toronto. 
 Diving into intimate personal archives and collective memory\, she writes 
 about queer friendship and queer desire. You can find her poems in\nCommo 
 Mag\,\nVoicemail Poem\ns\n\,\nGhost City Press’ My Loves: Digital Anthol
 ogy of Queer Love Poems\,\nand elsewhere.\nLateral Sway\nis her first book
 .
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College St.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF THE LAST STRAIGHT WOMAN BY PHOEBE MALTZ 
 BOVY
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260521T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260521T200000
UID:bm-5177720260521@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/0.jpg?cb=
 1777919509
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nJoin Flying Books and McClelland & Stewart for 
 the launch of\nThe Last Straight Woman\nby Phoebe Maltz Bovy in conversat
 ion with Kat Rosenfield.\nA fiercely intelligent\, funny\, and much-needed
  reality check on the state of heterosexual women.\nThe Last Straight Woma
 n i\ns a call to set aside the baggage of what female heterosexuality evok
 es\, in favour of a definition of what it actually entails: women liking m
 en. No more\, no less.\nThe Last Straight Woman\noffers a way forward for 
 straight women. In lieu of the sexy woman men are gazing at\, why not be t
 he woman too busy gazing at sexy men to have bothered with her own looks? 
 Phoebe offers not a self-improvement PowerPoint\, but rather an assortment
  of characters from 1990s British sitcoms\, a rare world where the frumpy 
 but horny straight lady had her day. She does not demand that 21st century
  women model themselves after supporting characters from long-forgotten te
 levision shows but rather merely consider the possibility that straight wo
 men are all one argyle sweater-vest away from true liberation.\nPHOEBE MAL
 TZ BOVY is a cultural critic in Toronto\, and the author of\nThe Perils of
  “Privilege”\n(St. Martins\, 2017). She is a regular columnist for the
 \nGlobe and Mail\,\na senior editor at\nThe Canadian Jewish News\,\nand a 
 co-host (with Kat Rosenfield) of the\nFeminine Chaos\npodcast. Her newslet
 ter\,\nClose-reading the Reruns\n\, relates contemporary North American is
 sues to 90s British sitcoms. She has written for\nThe Atlantic\,\nThe Forw
 ard\,\nand T\nhe New Republic\n\, among many other publications. Phoebe ha
 s a Ph.D. in French from New York University.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:MAKE IT MAKE SENSE: THE FLYING BOOKS NONFICTION BOOKCLUB
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260524T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260524T203000
UID:bm-5133520260524@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/make.png?
 cb=1776818187
DESCRIPTION:[Book Club]\nJoin Tomas Hachard\, author of\nCity in Flames\n\
 , on Sunday\, May 24\, from 6:30 to 8:30 pm for a lively discussion of\nHy
 perpolitics\nby Anton Jäger.\nWhat happens when politics is everywhere\, 
 yet nothing seems to change? From the abandoned dance floors of Thatcher
 ’s London to the mass mobilizations of Black Lives Matter\, Anton Jäger
  traces how pub­lic life has become infused with protest\, spectacle\, an
 d moral urgency — while the old infrastructure of parties\, unions\, and
  civic solidarity has been hollowed out.\nHyperpolitics\nrevisits the illu
 sions of the “end of history” and dissects the strange energies that r
 eplaced them: viral outrage\, endless culture wars\, and the digital rush 
 of causes that flare and vanish overnight. It explores why politics feels 
 constant\, yet change remains elusive.\nTomas Hachard published\nCity in F
 lames\nwith Flying Books in 2023. His short fiction has appeared in\nThe W
 alrus\n\, and he has written for\nThe Atlantic\, The Guardian\, The Globe 
 and Mail\, Slate\, Guernica Magazine\, The Literary Review of Canada\,\nTh
 e New Quarterly\, a\nnd\nHazlitt\,\namong others. He is also a former book
  and film critic for NPR.\nYour book club ticket includes:\nA glass of win
 e (or another beverage of your choice).\nA thoughtful conversation with ou
 r led by Tomas Hachard.\nA $20 credit toward any book in the shop.\nGET YO
 UR TICKETS HERE!\nA few details:\nTicket price includes HST and fees.\n$20
  book credit is valid on the night of attendance only (non-refundable and 
 non-transferable).\nWine is served on the night of the event (ID required)
 .\nTickets are non-refundable within 7 days of the event\; due to limited 
 capacity and planning\, we’re not able to transfer tickets to future boo
 k club sessions.
LOCATION:Flying Books at Neverland\, 371 Queen St. W.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF TEMPORARY PALACES BY JEFF MILLER
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260525T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260525T200000
UID:bm-4878620260525@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/May_25_Fl
 ying_Books.JPG?cb=1773686253
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nJoin House of Anansi and Flying Books to celebr
 ate the launch of\nTemporary Palace\ns by Jeff Miller. Jeff will be joined
  by authors Niko Stratis and Emma Healey.\n"A plainspoken\, punk-rock hymn
  … This is a book that will pick you up off the floor of a mosh pit and 
 wrap you in its arms." —Sean Michaels\nIn the fevered summer of 2001\, c
 harismatic activist Rob and his collective set up a squat in an abandoned 
 house. His bandmate and lover\, Ben\, watches anxiously as his own plans a
 re threatened by Rob’s choice of radical politics over music. Meanwhile\
 , photographer Alex finds herself torn between documenting the chaos of th
 e scene and saving the friendship that binds them together. When the polic
 e break up the squat\, Rob vanishes\, and the dream dies.\n\nTen years lat
 er\, Alex and Ben find each other again—she’s conquering Montreal’s 
 contemporary art world\, he’s running a thriving restaurant in Ottawa. B
 ut their success feels hollow. As they excavate their shared past\, they m
 ust confront the ghost of Rob’s disappearance and the trauma that pushed
  them apart.\n\nPulsing with the raw energy of basement punk shows and DIY
  creativity\, late-night manifestos and first heartbreaks\,\nTemporary Pal
 aces\nis a blazing debut that captures a generation caught between idealis
 m and survival\, art and activism\, the dreams that define us and the comp
 romises that save us.\nJEFF MILLER is the author of the award-winning crea
 tive nonfiction collection\nGhost Pine:\nA\nll Stories True.\nHis stories 
 have appeared in several anthologies\, and he frequently publishes critici
 sm. Jeff holds an MFA from the University of British Columbia and lives in
  Nova Scotia.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College St.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:FOUR POETS: LAURIE D. GRAHAM\, BARBARA TRAN\, KEVIN IRIE\, RONNA B
 LOOM
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260527T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260527T200000
UID:bm-5013320260527@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/POET.png?
 cb=1775483296
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nJoin four award-winning poets as they read from
  their newest books\nand discuss their work and the art and craft of poetr
 y. Books will be\navailable for sale and signing.\nLaurie D. Graham is a p
 oet\, an editor\, and the publisher of\nBrick\nmagazine. Her newest book i
 s\nCalling It Back to Me\n(McClelland &\nStewart).\nBarbara Tran’s debut
  poetry book\,\nPrecedented Parroting\n(Palimpsest Press)\, was selected a
 s a finalist for the Governor\nGeneral’s Literary Award and the Fred Cog
 swell Award for\nExcellence in Poetry.\nKevin Irie’s newest book is\nEva
 cuations\n(University of Alberta\nPress). His works have been translated i
 nto Spanish\, French\, and\nJapanese\, and his book\,\nViewing Tom Thomson
 : A Minority Report\,\nwas a finalist for the Acorn-Plantos Award and the 
 Toronto Book\nAward.\nRonna Bloom is the author of eight books of poetry\,
  most recently\nIn a Riptide\n(\nBrick Books). Her work has been broadcast
  on the CBC\,\nrecorded by the Canadian National Institute for the Blind\,
  translated\ninto Bangla and Chinese\, and shortlisted for several Canadia
 n literary\nawards.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College St.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE MCCLELLAND & STEWART'S AUTHOR SALON
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260528T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260528T220000
UID:bm-5215620260528@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/26_0272_M
 andSSalon_INSTAPOST_1080x1350.jpg?cb=1777649008
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nJoin McClelland & Stewart on Thursday\, May 28t
 h for their annual Author Salon\, as they celebrate their 120th anniversar
 y. In partnership with TIFA and Flying Books\, the McClelland & Stewart Au
 thor Salon is a gathering in celebration of their authors\, their readers\
 , and their community.\nA scathing and thought-provoking exploration of po
 wer and philanthropy\, Sharon Bala’s GOOD GUYS shows the dark side of ch
 arity\, and what happens when you try to change the world for all the wron
 g reasons.\n\nFearless and vulnerable\, UNSHAMING from Jowita Bydlowska tr
 anscends the typical self-help memoir and challenges the narrative surroun
 ding addiction\, exploring new ways to evolve the conversation.\n\nSet in 
 a near-future Kolkata ravaged by climate change and social disharmony\, Me
 gha Majumdar’s A GUARDIAN AND A THIEF follows five characters as their l
 ives collide in this propulsive and shattering tour de force.\n\nWith expe
 rt storytelling that will leave you high on passion and inspiration\, Mark
  Medley’s LIVE TO SEE THE DAY is a globe-trotting exploration of what dr
 ives certain people to pursue a dream.\n\nAchingly relatable and intensely
  funny\, Amil Niazi’s LIFE AFTER AMBITION looks at how the concept of am
 bition has shaped her and a generation of Millennials.\n\nAnd finally\, fi
 rst published in 1969\, THE EDIBLE WOMAN is Margaret Atwood’s brilliant 
 first novel\, introducing her as a consummate observer of the ironies and 
 absurdities of modern life.\nGET YOUR TICKETS HERE!
LOCATION:Paradise Theatre\, 1006 Bloor St W\, Toronto\,
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF SILVER LAKE BY ALEX PUGSLEY
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260601T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260601T200000
UID:bm-5232220260601@flyingbooks.ca
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nJoin Flying Books and Biblioasis for the launch
  of Alex Pugsley's new book\, Silver Lake!\nIt was on a fully dark Februar
 y night when I went to the Communist's Daughter to meet a friend who never
  showed that my life in movies began.\nSo begins the third installment in 
 a series of standalone novels about the life and travels of Aubrey McKee. 
 Set in Toronto and Silver Lake\, a creative neighbourhood in Los Angeles\,
  the novel chronicles with infectious élan Aubrey’s journey from broken
 -hearted derelict to B-movie production assistant\, comedy writer\, and sc
 ience-ﬁction screenwriter\, all the way up to feature ﬁlm director. Al
 ong the way\, he encounters long-ago childhood friends\, manic producers\,
  NHL players turned talk show hosts\, Victoria’s Secret models\, impulsi
 ve movie stars . . . and his own rising destiny.\nAlex Pugsley has worked 
 on over 185 produced episodes of television\, writing for performers such 
 as Lauren Ash\, Scott Thompson\, Dan Aykroyd\, and Michael Cera. He wrote 
 and directed the feature ﬁlm Dirty Singles which won for him the Irving 
 Avrich Emerging Filmmaker Award at TIFF. Following the publication of his 
 first novel\,\nAubrey McKee\,\nhe was named one of CBC’s Writers to Watc
 h. His ﬁrst story collection\,\nShimmer\,\nwas nominated for the ReLit A
 ward for Short Fiction\, and his most recent novel\,\nThe Education of Aub
 rey McKee\,\nwas long listed for the Toronto Book Awards. His next book\,\
 nThe Hungarian Ballroom\,\nan Aubrey McKee novella\, is forthcoming from B
 iblioasis. More information can be found at www.alexpugsley.com.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College Street
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR AN AUTHOR SOCIAL WITH LARISSA PHAM
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260603T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260603T203000
UID:bm-5216620260603@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Larissa_P
 ham_w_Marlowe_Author_Social.jpg?cb=1778080948
DESCRIPTION:[Author Social]\nJoin us for a conversation with Larissa Pham 
 author of\nDiscipline\n\, in conversation with Marlowe Granados\, Flying B
 ooks author of\nHappy Hour.\nAdmission is free\, but space is limited. Pl
 ease RSVP to attend.\nDiscipline\nis a taut\, electrifying debut about a 
 woman forced to confront unsettling truths about herself\, her past\, and 
 the life she rebuilt following a ruinous affair with her former mentor\, f
 rom “a writer to keep a close eye on” (\nThe New York Times Book Revie
 w\n)\n\nChristine is on tour for her novel\, a revenge fantasy based on a 
 real-life relationship gone bad with an older professor ten years prior. N
 ow on the road\, Christine is seeking answers—about how to live a good l
 ife and what it means to make art—through intimate conversations with st
 rangers\, past lovers\, and friends.\n\nBut when the antagonist of her nov
 el—her old painting professor—reaches out in a series of sly communiqu
 es after years of silence to tell her he's read her book\, Christine must 
 reckon with what it means to lose the reins of a narrative she wrote preci
 sely to maintain control. When her professor invites her to join him at hi
 s cabin\, deep in the woods of Maine\, what she encounters threatens to ch
 ange the very foundations of her life as she's imagined it.\n\nA delicatel
 y explosive high-wire act about the fictions we construct for ourselves ju
 st to survive\, Discipline is a terse triumph about art-making and rigor\,
  intimacy and attention\, punishment and release.\nLARISSA PHAM\nis the au
 thor of the essay collection\nPop Song\n\, a finalist for the National Boo
 k Critics Circle John Leonard prize. Her writing has appeared in\nGranta\n
 \,\nThe Nation\n\,\nThe New York Times Book Review\n\,\nBookforum\n\, and 
 elsewhere. She holds an MFA in fiction from the Bennington Writing Seminar
 s and teaches at the New School’s MFA in creative writing.\nDiscipline\n
 is her first novel.\nMARLOWE GRANADOS\nis a writer and filmmaker. She coho
 sts The Mean Reds\, a podcast dedicated to women-led films\, and her advic
 e column\, "Designs for Living\," appears in\nThe Baffler\n. After spendin
 g time in New York and London\, Granados currently resides in Toronto.\nHa
 ppy Hour\nis her debut novel.
LOCATION:Flying Books at Neverland\, 371 Queen St. W.
URL:https://flyingbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Flying Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:JOIN US FOR THE LAUNCH OF NIGHTBERRIES
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260604T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260604T200000
UID:bm-5167220260604@flyingbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8102716/Nightberr
 ies_Launch__1_.png?cb=1777570279
DESCRIPTION:[Book Launch]\nFlying Books\, CMU Press and River Street Writi
 ng are delighted to host the launch of\nNightberries\nby Elina Penner in c
 onversation with Sherri Klassen.\nDream or reality\, nightmare or trustwor
 thy memory?\nNightberrie\ns is a dark modern fairytale from German author 
 Elina Penner about the enigma who is Nelli Neufeld. The quiet youngest dau
 ghter in a noisy\, tangled German Mennonite family who fled from Russia in
  the 1990s\, Nelli seems to be coping well in a traditional marriage. But 
 what is going on in her memory? As we follow the events on two key days in
  2020\, we are asked riddles about Nelli that even she might not be able t
 o answer.\nA hauntingly original\, sometimes absurdly comic psychological 
 tale from contemporary Germany\,\nNightberrie\ns has been translated into 
 English by Bradley Schmidt from the 2022 novelNachtbeeren.\nElina Penner w
 as born in 1987 as a Mennonite German in the former Soviet Union and moved
  to Germany in 1991. Plautdietsch (Low German) is her mother tongue. After
  time spent in Berlin and the US\, she now lives with her family in the Ea
 st Westphalia region of Germany. She is a successful personal essayist and
  blogger\, with many of her non-fiction pieces gathered in the 2023 collec
 tion\nMigrantenmutti (Migrant Mother\n).\nNachtbeeren\nwas her first novel
 . In 2025\, her second novel\,\nDie Unbußfertigen (The Unrepentant\n)\, w
 as released in Germany.\nSherri (S.L.) Klassen is the author of\nMenno-Nig
 htcaps: Cocktails Inspired by that Odd Ethno-Religious Group You Keep Mist
 aking for the Amish\, Quakers or Mormons\n(Touchwood\, 2021. She has also 
 published in the\nConrad Grebel Review\nand\nThe Journal of Mennonite Writ
 ing\n. She was long known for her Mennonite-themed blog:\nThe Drunken Menn
 onite\n\, some pieces of which were reprinted in\nThe Anabaptist World Rev
 iew.\nShe has recently started writing on substack and has two novels in p
 rogress. She lives in Toronto and eats zwieback and verenijke from time to
  time\, but never verenijke made with nightberries.
LOCATION:Flying Books\, 784 College St.
